Covariates measured on the index stay:

CM vs non-CM;
AKI, diabetes and cirrhosis, from any diagnosis on the stay;
ICU admission (any ICU or CCU days);
procedures: lumbar puncture, other spinal, neurosurgical and lung procedures, and ventriculoperitoneal shunt. The shunt list is missing from both the supplement and the reconstruction (a copy/paste loss, in the maintainer’s judgment), so it is rebuilt from the official code sets: ICD-9-CM 02.34 and the nine ICD-10-PCS codes for bypass of a cerebral ventricle to the peritoneal cavity. Open: whether CPT codes, which the paper’s text names, are used as well as ICD procedure codes (#5).

Covariates measured at or before time zero:

age; sex and race (USRDS transplant file, record of the most recent transplant; race as White, Black or other); Hispanic ethnicity (patients file, format $YNUFMT: 1 or Y yes, 2 or N no, otherwise missing; the reconstruction treated code 2 as missing); Census region and division of residence (patients file STATE, FIPS codes; as in the reconstruction, Alaska and Hawaii are grouped with the territories as “Outside” rather than in the West, and no one is excluded by geography (decided in #12; the paper’s four regions sum to 1,257 of 1,265, consistent with an unshown Outside/unknown group). Table 1 shows Outside and Unknown as their own rows, subject to small-cell suppression (§10), so the region rows sum to the cohort);
BMI, pretransplant dialysis, HIV and CMV serostatus, from the UNOS registration of the most recent transplant on or before the index admission (decided 2026-11-10; the reconstruction used the first transplant’s registration);
number of transplants on or before, and graft failures before, the index admission;
time since the most recent transplant (also dichotomised at 2 years, as in the paper’s model);
acute rejection: any episode (UNOS format ACREJEPI 1 or 2; 3 is none; blank, 990 and 998 missing) at the most recent transplant’s registration or on its follow-up reports dated before the index admission (decided 2026-11-10). A follow-up report without a date takes the kidney graft failure date, as in the reconstruction;
any prior hospitalization with a transplant-complication code.

Immunosuppression:

antithymocyte globulin, alemtuzumab, anti-IL-2 receptor antibody, tacrolimus, cyclosporine, mycophenolate, mTOR inhibitors, azathioprine, steroids;
measured from the UNOS records in the USRDS transplant files, not Medicare drug claims (decided in #2):

at transplant: TXIRUNOS, linked to the recipient registration (TXUNOS_TRR_KI/_KP);
at first follow-up: TXIFUNOS, from the earliest follow-up report (TXFUUNOS_KI/_KP, earliest FUDATE), whether or not it precedes the index admission, as the paper’s Methods describe (“the first post-transplant visit”). Under OPTN rules this is normally the 6-month report. A drug counts at follow-up if it is current maintenance at that visit (RXMNTCUR);

the transplant and its first follow-up are those of the most recent transplant on or before the index admission (decided 2026-11-10); a registration with no drug records is missing, not “not on”;
drug classes come from RX_CD and DRUG_CD_OSTXT, using the reconstruction’s mapping, checked against Appendix C, with codes 57 (generic Myfortic), 58 (generic sirolimus) and 59 (Envarsus XR), which it missed, added (decided 2026-11-10).

The paper’s Discussion refers to a “1-year follow-up”, and its Methods and Limitations say the drugs came from “billing codes”; the plan follows the Methods’ time point and the UNOS records. The maintainer notes that billing data may be the gold standard, so this choice may be revisited (#2).

Codes for a concept
codes_for_concept() picks one concept’s codes out of the code_lists table, and can limit them to one code type: diagnosis, procedure or HCPCS. It stops with an error if the concept name is misspelled, rather than silently returning no codes.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Return the code list for one concept as a tibble with columns code and
# match ("exact" or "prefix"), optionally limited to one code type.
codes_for_concept &lt;- function(code_lists, concept_name, type = NULL) {
  selected &lt;- code_lists %&gt;%
    filter(concept == concept_name)

  if (!is.null(type)) {
    selected &lt;- selected %&gt;%
      filter(code_type == type)
  }

  if (nrow(selected) == 0) {
    stop(str_glue("No codes found for concept '{concept_name}'."), call. = FALSE)
  }

  selected %&gt;%
    select(code, match)
}

Searching wide code columns
The USRDS hospitalization files record each stay’s codes in separate columns:

the principal diagnosis, PRIMDIAG;
secondary diagnoses HSDIAG1 onwards (10 columns before 2010, 26 from 2010);
procedures HSSURG1 onwards.

flag_codes_wide() flags each row where any of the named columns holds a listed code. It skips columns a file does not have, so the same call works on both files (coverage steps 4–6 and 9).


Click to show/hide R Code
# usRds gap (coverage map, steps 4-6 and 9): the hospitalization files hold
# diagnoses and procedures in wide columns (PRIMDIAG, HSDIAG1-HSDIAG26,
# HSSURG1-HSSURG25). Returns TRUE for each row where any of `columns` matches
# `codes`. Columns that do not exist (HSDIAG11+ before 2010) are skipped.
flag_codes_wide &lt;- function(df, columns, codes) {
  present_columns &lt;- intersect(columns, names(df))

  if (length(present_columns) == 0) {
    stop("None of the requested code columns are in the data.", call. = FALSE)
  }

  any_match &lt;- rep(FALSE, nrow(df))
  for (column in present_columns) {
    any_match &lt;- any_match | code_matches(df[[column]], codes)
  }
  any_match
}

Flagging concepts on records
flag_concepts() adds one yes/no column per concept to hospitalization records. A record gets “yes” when any of the named columns holds a code from that concept’s list.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Add one logical column per concept to hospitalization records: TRUE when any
# of `columns` holds a code from that concept's list. Columns are named
# &lt;concept&gt; (for example, diabetes).
flag_concepts &lt;- function(records, columns, code_lists, concepts) {
  for (concept_name in concepts) {
    records[[concept_name]] &lt;- flag_codes_wide(
      records,
      columns,
      codes_for_concept(code_lists, concept_name)
    )
  }
  records
}

Demographic recodes
These turn the USRDS codes for sex, race and Hispanic ethnicity into labels. Hispanic ethnicity follows the official format, in which “no” is coded 2 or N. The reconstruction treated 2 as missing.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Sex and race (USRDS transplant file codes) and Hispanic ethnicity (patients
# file, format $YNUFMT: 1 or Y yes, 2 or N no, anything else missing).
recode_sex &lt;- function(code) {
  case_when(code == "1" ~ "Male", code == "2" ~ "Female", .default = NA)
}

recode_race &lt;- function(code) {
  case_when(
    code == "1" ~ "White",
    code == "2" ~ "Black",
    code %in% c("3", "4", "5", "6") ~ "Other",
    .default = NA
  )
}

recode_hispanic &lt;- function(code) {
  case_when(
    code %in% c("1", "Y") ~ "Hispanic",
    code %in% c("2", "N") ~ "Non-Hispanic",
    .default = NA
  )
}

Methods
The cohort is built from USRDS hospitalization files, Medicare claims and UNOS transplant records. The outcome is death from any cause within 180 days of the first cryptococcosis hospitalization after transplant. It is analysed with:

descriptive tables;
a Kaplan–Meier curve;
multivariable logistic regression.

Study design and methods explains the choices in plain language. The analysis plan is the authoritative specification. The usRds coverage page maps each step to the package.

Check the ggplot2 version
The theme relies on features added in ggplot2 4.0.0: a theme can carry the palettes its figures use, and labels can be looked up by variable name. On an older version the script stops with a message saying what to update, rather than failing later with a less helpful error.


Click to show/hide R Code
if (utils::packageVersion("ggplot2") &lt; "4.0.0") {
  stop(
    "The figures need ggplot2 4.0.0 or later; this R has ggplot2 ",
    utils::packageVersion("ggplot2"), ". Update it with install.packages()."
  )
}

Labels
label_figure() replaces column names with readable names, and figure_label() looks up one name for a title or axis label. Both take the labels as arguments, so the file reads nothing from the rest of the project. This study’s figures take their readable names from report_labels, defined on the Tables page.


Click to show/hide R Code
# Axis and legend titles from labels (readable names, named by variable), so
# no raw column name reaches a figure; arm names (named by level) in place of
# their codes in the legend and, with arm_axis = TRUE, on an arm x-axis
label_figure &lt;- function(labels, arm_labels, arm_axis = FALSE) {
  layers &lt;- list(
    ggplot2::labs(dictionary = labels),
    ggplot2::scale_color_discrete(labels = arm_labels)
  )
  if (arm_axis) {
    layers &lt;- c(layers, list(ggplot2::scale_x_discrete(labels = arm_labels)))
  }
  layers
}

# One variable's readable label from labels, or its name when it has none
figure_label &lt;- function(variable, labels = NULL) {
  if (variable %in% names(labels)) {
    unname(labels[[variable]])
  } else {
    variable
  }
}
